  <h1>India’s Water Crisis — Governance, Scarcity, and Sustainable Solutions</h1>
  <div><em>The Hindu | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>Water is emerging as one of India’s most pressing challenges. In 2024, The Hindu highlighted groundwater depletion, inter-state disputes, and erratic rainfall as major contributors to India’s looming water crisis. For UPSC, this issue represents the intersection of environment, economy, and governance.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Current Status<br/>•	Availability: Per capita water availability fell to ~1,486 cubic metres (2021) — “water-stressed” level (Falkenmark index).<br/>•	Groundwater Use: India uses 25% of global groundwater extraction.<br/>•	Agriculture: ~80% of freshwater withdrawn for irrigation.<br/>•	Urban Stress: Tier-2 cities facing summer water scarcity.<br/>•	Climate Change: IMD notes rising frequency of droughts and floods.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Key Challenges<br/>1.	Over-extraction of Groundwater: Particularly in Punjab, Haryana, Rajasthan.<br/>2.	Inefficient Irrigation: Flood irrigation dominates, micro-irrigation limited.<br/>3.	Pollution: Industrial effluents, untreated sewage contaminating rivers.<br/>4.	Inter-State Conflicts: Cauvery, Krishna, Ravi–Beas disputes.<br/>5.	Urban Mismanagement: Leakages in water supply >30%.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Government Measures<br/>•	Jal Jeevan Mission (2019): Target functional household tap connections by 2024.<br/>•	Atal Bhujal Yojana: Groundwater management in stressed states.<br/>•	Namami Gange Mission: River rejuvenation efforts.<br/>•	National Water Policy (2012): Integrated water resources management.<br/>•	PM Krishi Sinchai Yojana: Efficient irrigation and water-use practices.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Achievements<br/>•	Tap Connections: Over 13 crore households covered under Jal Jeevan Mission.<br/>•	Awareness: Growing citizen activism on water issues.<br/>•	Digital Monitoring: Remote sensing, groundwater mapping initiatives.<br/>•	Revival Efforts: Some states focusing on tank and lake rejuvenation.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Persistent Gaps<br/>•	Implementation Issues: Delays in rural water schemes.<br/>•	Equity Concerns: Rich farmers and industries overuse groundwater.<br/>•	Weak Regulation: Water a state subject → fragmented governance.<br/>•	Climate Vulnerability: Drought–flood cycles worsening stress.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Significance<br/>•	Economic Growth: Water critical for agriculture and industry.<br/>•	Public Health: Scarcity and contamination worsen disease burden.<br/>•	Social Stability: Water conflicts fuelling rural–urban tensions.<br/>•	Sustainability: Linked to SDG 6 (Clean Water & Sanitation).<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-I (Geography): Water resources, droughts, floods.<br/>•	GS-II (Governance): Centre–State disputes, welfare policies.<br/>•	GS-III (Environment): Water management, sustainability.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Water is the new oil of the 21st century.”<br/>o	“India’s water crisis is a governance crisis.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: Resource politics and federalism.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Groundwater Regulation: Metering, crop diversification, community-led monitoring.<br/>2.	Efficient Irrigation: Expand drip and sprinkler irrigation.<br/>3.	Urban Water Reforms: Reuse, rainwater harvesting, leakage reduction.<br/>4.	River Basin Planning: Interstate water-sharing based on science.<br/>5.	Behavioural Change: Public campaigns for water conservation.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s water crisis is man-made and governance-driven. Addressing it requires a shift from supply-side engineering to demand management, equity, and sustainability. For UPSC aspirants, this theme shows how environment, economy, and governance converge in resource politics.</p>
